# tools/t/tool_path_witness.rish -- the tools-fold resolver and its shape scan, proven on metal.
#
# WHY THIS RUNG. `tools/` stood many times over the 256 flat files the mark law names, past
# GitHub's 1,000-entry listing cap, so the room's web view was truncated for any reader. The count
# itself is deliberately absent from this comment and printed by the scan at run time instead: the
# room gained twenty-four entries in the hour this rung was first written, and a witness that
# recites a moving number goes stale inside the very commit that moves it (REDS %108, %152).
# Keaton's word `20260823.130056` chose the shape -- FIRST SPRIG LETTER, one letter deeper where a
# letter overflows -- and the move landed `20260823.144100`. It moved every flat entry and stopped
# every reference already written at a flat path from landing: 14,151 of them measured
# `20260823.124407`, of which 2,866 sit in dated testimony that keeps every word it ever wrote.
#
# The ranked remainder named the round's order plainly -- checkpoint, WRITE AND PROVE THE RESOLVER
# FIRST, move, repoint living, run the roster, and only then lower the bound roster to include
# `tools`. This rung was that second step and stood before anything moved; it now proves the same
# claims against the folded room, where the stale references are real rather than planted.
#
# WHAT IS PROVEN HERE, all of it on metal.
#
#   The verdicts, each on a real case in this tree today. A path the fold never touched still reads
#   `home`. A reference the fold made stale is recovered by the LETTER RULE, reaching the
#   two-letter room a split letter produced. A path written with leading `../` is rooted first and
#   then computed, which is the shape 204 of this tree's own references wear. A file living
#   somewhere the letter rule cannot predict is recovered by the index. And `amphora_lap1.rish`,
#   which genuinely stands at two paths inside `tools/`, reads `ambiguous` and names both rather
#   than choosing.
#
#   The letter rule works on a room that has never folded. A room is planted, one file placed at
#   `<room>/m/` and another at `<room>/ca/`, and the FLAT reference resolves to each by pure
#   computation from the basename -- no index consulted, no table of which letters split, no
#   prior knowledge of that room. This is the claim that made the fold safe: the transform from
#   old path to new is a pure function of the basename, so it keeps working for rooms not yet
#   folded and folds not yet run.
#
#   A bare sibling resolves inside its citer's own room, and refuses to guess without one. Two
#   tools sitting flat in one room, one naming the other with no directory at all, are exactly
#   what a fold separates. Given the citing file the room is read off it -- and a citer that has
#   itself been folded has its letter room stripped first, so the room recovered is the room
#   proper rather than one letter of it.
#
#   Four RED paths. A reference that resolves nowhere exits non-zero and names no home rather
#   than inventing one. A shared basename is never quietly collapsed to a single answer. A room
#   caught MID-MOVE, with both the one-letter and two-letter candidates standing, names both
#   rather than picking whichever was tested last. And every home the resolver names is checked
#   to actually exist -- a resolver returning a path which is not there would be worse than one
#   returning nothing.
#
#   The room's shape, read from the scan's own output rather than recited here (REDS %108). The
#   flat verdict passes, no computed room lands on a subdirectory already standing, and every room
#   the fold actually made stands under bound. That last reading is the one that keeps meaning
#   something now: a folded room has no flat entries, so a gate resting on the flat readings alone
#   would pass by having nothing left to measure (REDS %161's vacuous truth).
#
#   The scan can red, proven by a control that builds throwaway rooms and makes each gated reading
#   rise -- including a folded room whose standing letter room stands over bound, and the flat
#   verdict proven both `ok` and `red`. A guard that cannot red guards nothing, and a reading that
#   has only ever been zero is a reading nobody has seen work.
#
# WHAT THIS RUNG FOUND BEFORE THE MOVE, AND HOW THE MOVE ANSWERED IT. `tools/` held 24 flat
# symlinks, every one reaching `../crypto/` or `../tally/`. A fold moves each one directory deeper,
# where `../crypto/x25519.rye` resolves to `tools/crypto/x25519.rye` and is nowhere -- proven by
# moving a planted copy of exactly that shape, and `tools/t/tracked_link_witness.rish` gates
# dangling tracked symlinks at ZERO. The move round answered it by measurement rather than by
# choice: Zig refuses an import that escapes the root file's directory (*import of file outside
# module path*), so a bare-name `@import` is a directory relationship the language enforces, and
# every `.rye` source of `tools/` moved into `tools/rye/` together with the 24 shims and the
# `enrich/` room they import. Each shim target deepened one level to `../../crypto/`.
#
# WHAT IS NOT PROVEN. Nothing here moves a file or repoints a reference; the resolver reads and
# reports. `tools/fixtures/tool_path_repoint_scan.sh` does the repointing, and proves itself.
#
# Chapter: the tools fold -- the resolver, and the move it made safe.
# Kin: tools/t/tool_path_resolve.rish - tools/fixtures/tool_path_scan.sh -
# tools/fixtures/tool_path_control.sh - tools/fixtures/tool_path_repoint_scan.sh -
# tools/d/dated_path_resolve.rish (the sibling this follows) -
# .claude/rules/stamp-and-name.md - expanding-prompts/20260823-124407_the-ranked-remainder.md
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/t/tool_path_witness.rish

say "tool-path: Language EN -- a room past the bound a browser can list is a room nobody can enter, and a fold that breaks its own references costs more than the clutter it clears. This proves a stale tools reference resolves by computation, so the whole room may move without the tree losing its way back."
say "tool-path: Style Gauge at Meter -- five claims in order. The verdicts land on real cases, stale ones included. The letter rule recovers a room that has never folded. A bare sibling resolves inside its citer own room. Four RED paths refuse. And the room shape comes from the scan own output."
say "tool-path: Lens TAME -- safety first: the resolver stood BEFORE the move and answers the same way after it, every named home is checked to exist, every number is read from a scan rather than recited, and the scan is proven able to red by a control that makes each reading rise."

let bin = "rishi/bin/rishi"
# Repointed by hand on `20260823.144100`. The repointer excludes `tool_path_*` so the deliberately
# stale citations below survive its apply run, and the price of that exclusion is exactly this: the
# file's own LIVE references are the one set no tool will move for it.
let tool = "tools/t/tool_path_resolve.rish"
let scan = "tools/fixtures/tool_path_scan.sh"
let control = "tools/fixtures/tool_path_control.sh"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PART ONE -- the verdicts, each on a real case standing in this tree today.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# A path that never moved: `tools/fixtures/` was a subdirectory before the fold and is one after,
# so its citations are as true today as they were yesterday and must read `home` unchanged.
let standing_ref = "tools/fixtures/tool_path_scan.sh"
let standing = run [bin "run" tool standing_ref]
assert standing.ok else "tool-path: a tool standing at its own path must resolve"
assert standing.out contains "verdict=home" else "tool-path: a path that still stands must read home rather than being recovered"
assert standing.out contains "home=${standing_ref}" else "tool-path: a standing path must resolve to itself"
say "verdict one GREEN -- a path the fold never touched still reads home, unchanged"

# THE CASE THIS WHOLE RUNG WAS BUILT FOR, and it is a real one now rather than a planted one. Every
# document, log, and commit written before `20260823.144100` cites `tools/caravan_suite_witness.rish`
# at the flat path the room no longer has. The letter rule computes the answer from the basename
# alone: `c`, then `ca` when `c` misses, with the two-letter rooms TRIED rather than looked up.
let stale_ref = "tools/caravan_suite_witness.rish"
let stale = run [bin "run" tool stale_ref]
assert stale.ok else "tool-path: a reference the fold made stale must still resolve"
assert stale.out contains "verdict=recovered-by-letter-rule" else "tool-path: a stale flat reference must be recovered by computation rather than by index"
assert stale.out contains "home=tools/ca/caravan_suite_witness.rish" else "tool-path: the letter rule must reach the two-letter room a split letter produced"
say "verdict two GREEN -- a reference the fold made stale is recovered by pure computation from its basename"

# A path written with leading `../`, the way this tree's rules and foundations cite tools
# throughout -- 204 such references stand in the tracked collection. The `../` is dropped before
# the room is read, so the letter rule reaches this shape rather than falling through to the index.
let relative_ref = "../../tools/exec_bit_witness.rish"
let relative = run [bin "run" tool relative_ref]
assert relative.ok else "tool-path: a root-relative reference must resolve with its leading ../ dropped"
assert relative.out contains "verdict=recovered-by-letter-rule" else "tool-path: a root-relative reference must reach the letter rule rather than the index"
assert relative.out contains "home=tools/e/exec_bit_witness.rish" else "tool-path: the root-relative reading must land at the repository root"
say "verdict three GREEN -- a reference written with leading ../ is rooted first, then computed"

# A file living somewhere the letter rule cannot predict. `dated_path_scan.sh` sits in
# `tools/fixtures/`, so the letter rule computes `tools/d/` and misses, and the index recovers it.
let moved_ref = "tools/dated_path_scan.sh"
let moved = run [bin "run" tool moved_ref]
assert moved.ok else "tool-path: a file the letter rule cannot predict must still be recovered"
assert moved.out contains "verdict=recovered-by-index" else "tool-path: a file outside the letter rule must be recovered by the index"
assert moved.out contains "home=tools/fixtures/dated_path_scan.sh" else "tool-path: the index must name the file actual home"
say "verdict four GREEN -- a file the letter rule cannot predict is recovered by the index"

# A basename genuinely living at two paths inside `tools/` itself. This is a real case rather
# than a planted one, and it is exactly the shape a fold multiplies.
let shared_ref = "amphora_lap1.rish"
let shared = run [bin "run" tool shared_ref]
assert shared.ok else "tool-path: a shared basename must still report"
assert shared.out contains "verdict=ambiguous" else "tool-path: a basename at two paths must read ambiguous"
assert shared.out contains "home=tools/am/amphora_lap1.rish tools/gen/amphora/amphora_lap1.rish" else "tool-path: an ambiguous reading must name every home it found"
say "verdict five GREEN -- a basename at two paths inside tools reads ambiguous and names both"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PART TWO -- the letter rule proven on a room that has NEVER folded. This is the claim that
# makes the fold safe: the transform is a pure function of the basename, so it works for rooms
# this tree has not created yet -- including `tools/` on the day after the move.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

let proof_room = ".tool-path-proof"
let one_base = "mycelium_probe_witness.rish"
let two_base = "caravan_probe_witness.rish"
let one_flat = "${proof_room}/${one_base}"
let two_flat = "${proof_room}/${two_base}"
let one_home = "${proof_room}/m/${one_base}"
let two_home = "${proof_room}/ca/${two_base}"

let planted = run ["sh" "-c" "rm -rf .tool-path-proof && mkdir -p .tool-path-proof/m .tool-path-proof/ca && echo planted > .tool-path-proof/m/mycelium_probe_witness.rish && echo planted > .tool-path-proof/ca/caravan_probe_witness.rish"]
assert planted.ok else "tool-path: the planted room must be created before it can be resolved"

let one_letter = run [bin "run" tool one_flat]
let two_letter = run [bin "run" tool two_flat]

# The bare sibling: no room in the reference at all, and a citing file that has itself been
# folded into a letter room. The citer's letter room is stripped to recover the room proper.
let folded_citer = "${proof_room}/ca/${two_base}"
let sibling = run [bin "run" tool one_base folded_citer]
let orphan = run [bin "run" tool one_base]

# The mid-move case: both candidates standing at once, which no correct fold produces and which
# a half-finished one does. Planted after the readings above so it disturbs none of them.
let mid_move = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p .tool-path-proof/c && echo planted > .tool-path-proof/c/caravan_probe_witness.rish"]
assert mid_move.ok else "tool-path: the mid-move room must be created before it can be read"
let both = run [bin "run" tool two_flat]

let swept = run ["rm" "-rf" proof_room]
assert swept.ok else "tool-path: the planted room must be swept whether the proof passed or failed"

assert one_letter.ok else "tool-path: a flat reference into a one-letter room must resolve"
assert one_letter.out contains "verdict=recovered-by-letter-rule" else "tool-path: a room the resolver has never seen must be recovered by computation"
assert one_letter.out contains "home=${one_home}" else "tool-path: the letter rule must compute the room from the basename alone"
say "letter rule GREEN -- a flat reference into a never-before-seen one-letter room resolves by computation"

assert two_letter.ok else "tool-path: a flat reference into a two-letter room must resolve"
assert two_letter.out contains "verdict=recovered-by-letter-rule" else "tool-path: a two-letter room must be recovered by computation too"
assert two_letter.out contains "home=${two_home}" else "tool-path: the two-letter room must be computed rather than looked up"
say "two-letter GREEN -- a room split one letter deeper is TRIED rather than looked up, so no table of split letters can go stale"

assert sibling.ok else "tool-path: a bare sibling must resolve when its citing file is given"
assert sibling.out contains "verdict=recovered-by-letter-rule" else "tool-path: a bare sibling must be recovered inside its citing file own room"
assert sibling.out contains "home=${one_home}" else "tool-path: the sibling must resolve into its own letter room, with the citer letter room stripped"
assert (orphan.ok == false) else "tool-path: the same bare reference without a citer must refuse"
say "sibling GREEN -- a bare sibling resolves inside its citer own room, and refuses to guess without one"

# THE SECOND FOLDED ROOM -- glow/gen folded by letter on 20260828 (REDS %301), and the same pure
# rule answers it with no edit to the resolver: the room is read off the reference, the letter
# off the basename. A stale flat desk reference from before that fold recovers by computation.
let gen_stale = run [bin "run" tool "glow/gen/shape-amount.glow"]
assert gen_stale.ok else "tool-path: a stale flat glow/gen reference must resolve"
assert gen_stale.out contains "verdict=recovered-by-letter-rule" else "tool-path: glow/gen must be recovered by the letter rule, never the index"
assert gen_stale.out contains "home=glow/gen/s/shape-amount.glow" else "tool-path: the glow/gen letter room must be computed from the basename alone"
say "glow/gen GREEN -- the second letter-folded room answers by the same computation, resolver unedited"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PART THREE -- the RED paths. A resolver is trustworthy only where it refuses.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

let absent = run [bin "run" tool "tools/no_such_tool_was_ever_written.rish"]
assert (absent.ok == false) else "tool-path: a reference that resolves nowhere must exit non-zero"
assert absent.out contains "verdict=missing" else "tool-path: a reference that resolves nowhere must say so by name"
assert absent.out contains "home=-" else "tool-path: a missing reference must name no home rather than invent one"
say "RED one GREEN -- a reference that resolves nowhere refuses, and invents nothing"

assert (shared.out contains "verdict=recovered-by-index" == false) else "tool-path: a shared basename must never be quietly answered as though it were unique"
assert (shared.out contains "verdict=home" == false) else "tool-path: a shared basename must never be answered as though the reference still stood"
say "RED two GREEN -- a shared basename is never collapsed to one answer"

assert both.ok else "tool-path: a mid-move room must still report"
assert both.out contains "verdict=ambiguous" else "tool-path: with both candidates standing, the resolver must refuse to choose"
assert both.out contains "${proof_room}/c/${two_base}" else "tool-path: the mid-move reading must name the one-letter candidate"
assert both.out contains "${proof_room}/ca/${two_base}" else "tool-path: the mid-move reading must name the two-letter candidate"
say "RED three GREEN -- a room caught mid-move names both candidates rather than picking the last one tested"

# Every home named above is checked to actually exist. A resolver returning a path which is not
# there would be worse than one returning nothing, so the claim is spent rather than assumed.
let homes = [standing_ref "tools/ca/caravan_suite_witness.rish" "tools/e/exec_bit_witness.rish" "tools/fixtures/dated_path_scan.sh" "tools/am/amphora_lap1.rish" "tools/gen/amphora/amphora_lap1.rish"]
let missing_homes = where homes as h: ((run ["test" "-e" h]).ok == false)
assert (length missing_homes) == 0 else "tool-path: every home the resolver names must exist on disk"
say "RED four GREEN -- every home named by a verdict above stands on disk"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PART FOUR -- the fold's own preconditions, read from the scan rather than recited here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

let shape = run ["sh" scan "tools" "256"]
assert shape.ok else "tool-path: the fold-shape scan must run over tools/"

say "the tools room, measured by the scan rather than remembered:"
say shape.out

assert shape.out contains "flat_shape_gate=ok" else "tool-path: a room still holding flat entries must pass all three flat gates -- under bound, no collision, every basename long enough to slice"
assert shape.out contains "collisions=0" else "tool-path: no computed letter room may land on a subdirectory already standing"
assert shape.out contains "standing_rooms_over_bound=0" else "tool-path: every room the fold actually made must sit under the 256 bound"
say "preconditions GREEN -- the flat shape gate passes, no collision with a standing subdirectory, and every room the fold made stands under bound"

# WHY THE GATE MOVED, and it is the reason this rung would otherwise have started passing for the
# wrong reason. `min_basename_safe` and `rooms_over_bound` describe a room that still has flat
# entries to fold. `tools/` folded on `20260823.144100`, so its flat count is zero, every flat
# reading fell to zero with it, and a gate resting on those alone would pass by having nothing left
# to measure -- vacuous truth, the exact shape REDS %161 named. So the scan decides the flat
# question itself and reports `flat_shape_gate`, and the standing rooms are gated in their own
# right, which is the reading that keeps meaning something the day after a fold.

# THREE GATES, AND THE REST REPORTED. Only the readings above are asserted, because only those
# three decide whether the fold is safe. `flat_files`, `letters`, `over_bound_letters`, and
# `symlinks_up` are printed and left free: each moves with ordinary growth, and a guard that
# reds when someone adds a tool would stop the line for nothing. The scan prints them on every
# run, so the move round reads today's numbers rather than yesterday's pin.
#
# The symlink reading is the one the fold round must answer before it moves anything. The harm
# it warns of is already gated elsewhere -- `tools/tracked_link_witness.rish` holds dangling
# tracked symlinks at ZERO -- so this line is advance notice rather than a second wall.
say "ANSWERED BY THE MOVE ROUND (20260823.144100) -- the 24 up-reaching symlinks moved into tools/rye/ beside the .rye sources that import them, each target deepened one level to ../../crypto/. Zig refuses an import that escapes the root file's directory, so a bare-name @import is a directory relationship the language enforces and the whole Rye closure of tools/ is one room by the compiler's own rule. tracked_link stands green."

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PART FIVE -- the scan proven able to red, by a control that makes each reading rise.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

let controlled = run ["sh" control]
assert controlled.ok else "tool-path: the control must build its rooms and prove every behavior"
assert controlled.out contains "clean_room=rests" else "tool-path: a clean room must leave every gated reading at zero"
assert controlled.out contains "overflow_room=reds" else "tool-path: a letter over bound at both depths must raise rooms_over_bound"
assert controlled.out contains "collision_room=reds" else "tool-path: a computed room landing on a standing subdirectory must raise collisions"
assert controlled.out contains "reaching_room=reds" else "tool-path: an up-reaching symlink must raise symlinks_up"
assert controlled.out contains "absent_room=refuses" else "tool-path: the scan must refuse a room that is not there"
assert controlled.out contains "folded_room=reds_on_the_standing_reading" else "tool-path: a standing room over bound must raise standing_rooms_over_bound"
assert controlled.out contains "flat_shape_gate=proven_both_ways" else "tool-path: the flat shape verdict must be proven ok and red, so ok is never merely an absent reading"
assert controlled.out contains "behaviors_proven=7" else "tool-path: all seven control behaviors must be proven, not merely attempted"
say "control GREEN -- seven behaviors on throwaway rooms: one rests, four red, the flat verdict proven both ways, one refuses"

say "tool-path: GREEN -- the resolver stood before the move and answers the same way after it. Five verdicts on real cases including a reference the fold itself made stale, the letter rule proven on a room that has never folded, a bare sibling resolved inside its citer own room, four RED paths refusing, the room shape read from the scan, and the scan itself proven able to red on seven behaviors."
